            <h1 class="jw-heading-200" style="text-align: left;">NexGen Air Battlespace</h1>
<p>NOTE: <em>The non-factual content expressed in any part of this project represent personal opinions of the ACES Technology Team and do not represent an official statement on behalf of any organization.</em></p>
<h3>Introduction</h3>
<p>In this project, we aim provide an informative visualization of the complex network that is modern combat battlespace of the United States' operatives. Of the many new technologies and platforms that have recently been developed -- and new designs are slated to come out soon -- their individual capabilities and collective interactions are&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h3>Format</h3>
<p>The data we gather will be presented in a strategic and organized manner. The format will primarily be an online, three-dimensional, interactive visualization, with auxiliary profile cards for more detail on individual objects. If possible, a physical depiction of some models will be created. This depiction would be on display at the Alpha Conference hosted by ACES Technology, and possibly others.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h3>An Invitation to the Defense Industry</h3>
<p>As this project is heavily reliant on both descriptive and visual information, it is critical that we get both accurate and unclassified information. At the same time, we do not want to commit any copyright or license fraud in any way. Thus, we invite members of the defense industry as well as military contractors to send us any information they wish at their disposal. Three-dimensional models of designs, especially, would be greatly appreciated. A model need not be so detailed as to jeopardize the use and purpose of the parent design, whether to hinder its credit, compromise its secrecy, or hamper its innovation by sending a scale replica or 3D file of some sort to a private team. Nevertheless, the inclusion of such a model or information mentioned previously is to the advantage of the contractors, for our NexGen Air Battlespace provides a visualization for how the designs of the contractors fit in the larger picture of air combat network as used by the Armed Forces and Department of Defense. In our NexGen Air Battlespace project, we demonstrate to both the public at large and other, smaller industry members with whom contractors may wish to partner to develop the designs. We show the importance of the advanced technology displayed in the designs, providing details regarding individual capabilities of the platforms. Therefore, it is in the interest of the contractors for the promotion of their most excellent work.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h3>Content: What to Include</h3>
<p>It is obvious that not every aspect or detail of combat aircraft, their capabilities, and their purposes can be included. Thus, we must reduce the near-infinite possibilities to those that would seem most beneficial, primarily due to relevancy:</p>
<ul>
<li>The future of combat aircraft: Next-Generation Air Dominance (NGAD), the Skyborg program, F/A-XX, Collaborative Combat Aircraft (CCAs)</li>
<li>Specific scenes representing present, near-future wars and air conflicts: Ukraine and Russia, Israel and Hamas, China over the South China Sea</li>
<li>The dawn of Artificial Intelligence: X-62A VISTA, F-16 VENOM project</li>
<li>Designs from private companies, such as Heron's Chimera engine and the Quarterhorse designs</li>
</ul>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h3>Objects and Entities</h3>
<ul>
<li>Collaborative Combat Aircraft (CCA)</li>
<li>Link 16 (and others)</li>
<li>Next Generation Air Dominance (NGAD) fighters</li>
<li>The U.S. Navy's F/A-XX fighter(s)</li>
<li>Low-cost platforms</li>
<li>Quarterhorse Mk 0, Mk1
<ul>
<li>Though not combat aircraft in and of themselves, these designs are fundamental to the new developments of subsonic-supersonic-hypersonic platforms, with heavily anticipated prospects for combat use.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>    </div>
